From b2bb7773dca8a6126921e1db15ac2b58f82dd7f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: spiritedman4 <sivamsrini214@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 07:23:15 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Apply a remote move as a move, not a delete plus a create

Moving files into a different folder on one machine duplicated them on the
other: the second machine downloaded the files at their new path but kept
the originals at the old one, and re-uploaded them, undoing the move on
Drive.

Diff is keyed by path, so a move arrives as two unrelated facts -- a file
gone from one path, a file appeared at another. Judged separately the old
path is a deletion and the new path a creation. That is survivable while
both machines' baselines agree, but the delete half removes the baseline
record for the old path, and a machine that still holds the file there then
has a local file with no baseline: decideNoBaseline reads it as brand new
and uploads it, resurrecting the move's source. The other machine downloads
that back, and the duplicate is permanent on both sides.

Diff now detects moves before per-path judgement and emits a single
DecisionMoveLocal, which renames the file on disk and carries its baseline
record across. The old path never loses its record, so nothing can resurrect
it, and content already on disk is not downloaded again.

Matching is by Drive file ID: it is the only identity that survives a move,
since local hashes are sha256 while Drive reports md5, so content cannot be
compared across the boundary. Only unambiguous moves qualify -- destination
untracked and unoccupied locally, source still on disk, content unchanged on
both sides. A move tangled up with an edit falls back to the ordinary rules,
which are conservative and do not lose data.

Also prunes directories emptied by a move or a remote deletion. Nothing else
ever removed them, since the engine tracks files and not directories, so
every move left its source folder behind for good.

Not addressed: a machine whose baseline was reset still has files with no
record and cannot tell a leftover from a genuinely new file, so it will
upload them. Nothing in the manifest distinguishes the two, and local
sha256 cannot be matched against Drive's md5 to infer it from content.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
